  <section id="latest-national">
    <h2>EIA’s 2024 national snapshot</h2>
    <p>The U.S. Energy Information Administration reported that U.S. electricity customers experienced an average of <strong>11 hours of interruptions in 2024</strong>, nearly twice the annual average of the prior decade. EIA attributed <strong>80% of the 2024 hours without electricity to major events</strong>, including Hurricanes Beryl, Helene, and Milton. Interruptions attributed to major events averaged nearly nine hours in 2024, compared with nearly four hours per year on average from 2014 through 2023.</p>
    <p>These are EIA’s reported U.S. averages for one year that included major weather events. They do not show that every customer lost power for 11 hours, nor do they establish a trend for every utility or address.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="metrics">
    <h2>What SAIDI and SAIFI mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>SAIDI</strong> (System Average Interruption Duration Index) measures the total duration of non-momentary interruptions experienced by an average customer over a year.</li>
      <li><strong>SAIFI</strong> (System Average Interruption Frequency Index) measures the number of interruptions in a year.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These indices summarize utility-reported reliability information. Definitions, data coverage, and whether major events are included matter when comparing years or jurisdictions. Read EIA’s notes alongside the table rather than treating a single figure as a household prediction.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>What national or state averages cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>An annual average cannot predict your next outage’s timing, duration, cause, or restoration sequence. It does not tell you whether a particular home needs a generator, what loads should be backed up, what a project will cost, or whether an installation would pay for itself. A state-level statistic can mask substantial variation among utilities and service areas.</p>
    <p>We do not publish generator-ownership percentages, future payback claims, unsupported cause-of-outage shares, or a permanent “best/worst state” ranking. The EIA data cited here do not establish those claims.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="local-planning">
    <h2>Use local data and household needs for planning</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ask your electric utility for outage history or reliability information for your service area where available.</li>
      <li>Consider household-specific needs such as a private well, sump pump, communications, heating/cooling, or equipment that requires a continuity plan.</li>
      <li>Decide which circuits need backup and which loads can be delayed or excluded.</li>
      <li>Discuss the site, electrical service, fuel supply, safe transfer equipment, and installation permits with a qualified local professional.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use the <a href="/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">load-based sizing guide</a> and <a href="/cost/generator-roi-outage-costs/">cautious value framework</a> as planning context, not automated recommendations.</p>
  </section>
